{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg-2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Keeper of Gannet Rock\n\nEvery evening for thirty-one years, Edwin Marrow had climbed the one hundred and twelve iron steps of Gannet Rock Light, wound the clockwork that turned the great lens, and checked the wick. Tonight the wind came in from the northwest, and the sea below the tower had the color of pewter left out in the rain.\n\nHis daughter Ruth had written from the mainland that the Board was closing the station. Automation, she said. A buoy with a battery would do the work of three men and a bell. Edwin had read the letter twice at the kitchen table, then folded it into the pocket of his oilskin coat, where it still rested against his ribs like a stone.\n\nHe lit the lamp at dusk, as always. The flame caught slowly, then steadied, and the lens gathered it into a beam that swept across the water and out to the fishing grounds beyond the reef. He watched it cross the dark, once, twice, three times, the way he had watched it ten thousand times before.\n\nBelow him, the radio crackled. A voice he knew, old Tom Pellan, skipper of the *Mary Jane*, was out past the shoals with a load of cod and a bad engine.\n\n\"Gannet, Gannet, this is *Mary Jane*. Do you have me?\"\n\nEdwin keyed the handset. \"I have you, Tom. Bearing two-seven-zero, about four miles. Keep the light on your starboard bow and you'll clear the Teeth.\"\n\n\"Copy that. Thank you, Edwin.\"\n\nThere was a long hiss of static, then Tom's voice again, quieter. \"They say you're leaving us.\"\n\n\"They say a lot of things.\"\n\n\"Is it true?\"\n\nEdwin looked out through the thick glass at the spinning beam. \"Light's been burning here since before my father was born. Somebody will keep it burning. Maybe not a man. But somebody.\"\n\nThe radio went silent. Edwin imagined Tom nodding in the wheelhouse, his weathered hands steady on the wheel.\n\nLater, when the moon rose and silvered the water, Edwin climbed the steps once more and stood at the gallery rail. The wind was cold and clean. Far below, he could see the faint glow of the *Mary Jane*'s running lights moving toward the harbor, slow and certain, following the path his light had shown.\n\nHe thought of all the nights he had kept watch, and of the boats that had come home because someone had been awake to guide them. He thought of the buoy with its battery and its patient blinking, and found, to his surprise, that he did not resent it. A light was a promise, after all, and promises could be kept by many hands.\n\nRuth would be waiting for him on the mainland in a month's time. She had already made up the spare room.\n\nEdwin turned back toward the lamp room, where the great lens hummed softly in the dark, throwing its gold across the waves. He placed one hand on the cool brass of the rail, then went inside to check the wick one more time."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":962,"cache_read_input_tokens":4215,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":962,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu-1","agent_id":"tid-1","session_id":"sess-1","uuid":"uuid-49","timestamp":"2026-11-24T01:39:26.870Z","request_id":"apireq-2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write lighthouse keeper story"}
