ned Tom nodding in the wheelhouse, his weathered hands steady on the wheel.\n\nLater, when the moon rose and silvered the water, Edwin climbed the steps once more and stood at the gallery rail. The wind was cold and clean. Far below, he could see the faint glow of the *Mary Jane*'s running lights moving toward the harbor, slow and certain, following the path his light had shown.\n\nHe thought of all the nights he had kept watch, and of the boats that had come home because someone had been awake to guide them. He thought of the buoy with its battery and its patient blinking, and found, to his surprise, that he did not resent it. A light was a promise, after all, and promises could be kept by many hands.\n\nRuth would be waiting for him on the mainland in a month's time. She had already made up the spare room.\n\nEdwin turned back toward the lamp room, where the great lens hummed softly in the dark, throwing its gold across the waves. He placed one hand on the cool brass of the rail, then went inside to check the wick one more time.","usage":{"total_tokens":6135,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":6979},"uuid":"uuid-51","session_id":"sess-1"}
